 ## Overview
-The Exponential Approximation Method (EAM) is a direct volume ray casting technique that approximates the continuous light attenuation integral using discrete steps and an exponential transmittance model [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from standard linear alpha compositing by scaling sampled alpha values by a step-size-dependent correction factor, allowing larger steps while approximating exponential decay [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is typically chosen when performance is prioritized over exact physical accuracy, or when the volume data is sparse enough that early ray termination provides significant speedup [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The Exponential Approximation Method (EAM) is a direct volume ray casting technique that approximates the continuous light attenuation integral using discrete steps and an exponential transmittance model [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from standard linear alpha compositing by scaling sampled alpha values by a step-size-dependent correction factor, allowing larger steps while approximating exponential decay [S1, S2, unofficial]. It terminates early when accumulated alpha exceeds 0.99 [S2, unofficial]. It builds on [Ray Marching and Discretization](2788-ray-marching-and-discretization.md) and is closely related to [Front-to-Back Volume Rendering](2350-front-to-back-volume-rendering.md), [Fixed Step Ray Marching](3136-fixed-step-ray-marching.md), and [Volume Ray Casting](3161-volume-ray-casting.md).
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Input:** A 3D volume texture (scalar density field), a transfer function (mapping density to color and opacity), camera parameters (unprojected ray endpoints `vRayFrom` and `vRayTo`), and rendering parameters (`uStepSize`, `uAlphaCorrection` or `uExtinction`, `uOffset`) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Input:** A 3D volume texture (scalar density field), a transfer function (mapping density to color and opacity), camera parameters (`vRayFrom`, `vRayTo`), and rendering parameters (`uStepSize`, `uAlphaCorrection` or `uExtinction`, `uOffset`) [S1, S2, unofficial].
 *   **Output:** A 2D image pixel (RGBA color) [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
